# 3.2 — Content schema

**Layer:** 3 — Content · **Depends on:** 3.1 · **Blocks:** 3.4, 4.2, 4.3

## Objective

Turn the case-study format into an editorial contract. A case study cannot enter the build without a problem, a response, named deliverables, an outcome, and a verification source for every factual claim. The schema makes it structurally impossible to publish a screenshot with no story and prevents the omission that weakened the studied source material: thirteen case studies that never answered “did it work?”

The implementation has two gates. TypeScript rejects malformed authoring at compile time. Runtime validation rejects missing fields, empty strings, placeholder copy, broken pronoun partitioning, and unverifiable claims before `next build` starts. Editorial judgments that cannot be proved mechanically remain explicit manual-review flags rather than false guarantees.

## Prerequisites

- 3.1 has removed fabricated claims and identified the files that will become typed case-study data.
- The Next.js 14 App Router project from 1.2 builds with strict TypeScript.
- Every factual claim intended for publication has one confirmation method: a checked live URL, a checked public repository, or a dated user confirmation.
- The implementation has an agreed source directory: `content/case-studies/`.

## Steps

### 1. Define one closed editorial model

Create `content/schema.ts`. Keep the required narrative fields flat except for `verified`: the page template should project the editorial sequence directly. `technologies` remains separate from `response` so the template can quarantine the stack in a closing verbless fragment.

`verified` is a non-empty array of claim records. Each record pairs the factual claim with a discriminated verification source. A claim with no source cannot be represented as valid content.

```ts
// content/schema.ts
export type VerificationSource =
  | {
      kind: 'live-url'
      url: string
      checkedOn: string
    }
  | {
      kind: 'public-repo'
      url: string
      checkedOn: string
    }
  | {
      kind: 'user-confirmed'
      note: string
      confirmedOn: string
    }

export interface VerifiedClaim {
  claim: string
  source: VerificationSource
}

export interface CaseStudyFigure {
  src: string
  alt: string
  caption: string
}

export interface CaseStudyFootnote {
  marker: string
  text: string
}

export interface CaseStudyExternalLink {
  label: string
  url: string
}

export interface CaseStudy {
  slug: string
  tagline: string
  problem: string
  response: string
  deliverables: string[]
  technologies: string[]
  outcome: string
  verified: [VerifiedClaim, ...VerifiedClaim[]]
  figures?: CaseStudyFigure[]
  footnotes?: CaseStudyFootnote[]
  externalLinks?: CaseStudyExternalLink[]
  year?: number
}
```

Do not add `stats`, `impact`, `metrics`, `tags`, or an untyped metadata bag. New editorial concepts require named fields and validators.

### 2. Author data against the interface

Export the inventory from `content/case-studies/index.ts` with `satisfies readonly CaseStudy[]`. `satisfies` checks every object without widening useful literals.

```ts
// content/case-studies/index.ts
import type { CaseStudy } from '../schema'

export const caseStudies = [
  // Real, verified case-study objects land in 3.3 and 3.4.
] satisfies readonly CaseStudy[]
```

Do not add illustrative fake objects to make the array look complete. An empty array is valid while ground truth is pending; an invented example is not.

### 3. Add compiling runtime validation

Create `content/validate.ts`. The validator accepts `unknown`, accumulates all failures, and throws one error containing every content location. Throwing is mandatory: warnings alone do not block publication.

The placeholder detector is deliberately conservative and case-insensitive. It catches known scaffold language and common authoring markers without banning ordinary words such as “later” in legitimate prose.

```ts
// content/validate.ts
import type {
  CaseStudyExternalLink,
  CaseStudyFigure,
  CaseStudyFootnote,
  VerifiedClaim,
  VerificationSource,
} from './schema'

export interface ContentValidationResult {
  warnings: string[]
}

const PLACEHOLDER =
  /(?:\b(?:todo|tbd|tk)\b|lorem ipsum|placeholder|coming soon|featured project title|concise summary|insert (?:copy|text|content) here)/i

const SLUG = /^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/
const ISO_DATE = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
const RESPONSE_OPENING = /^I\s+(built|designed|wrote|structured|cut|rebuilt)\b/

// Approximation only. English finite-verb detection requires syntax and context;
// this list catches the likeliest violations and sends uncertain cases to review.
const TAGLINE_FINITE_VERBS = new Set([
  'am',
  'is',
  'are',
  'was',
  'were',
  'be',
  'been',
  'being',
  'has',
  'have',
  'had',
  'does',
  'do',
  'did',
  'builds',
  'designs',
  'writes',
  'structures',
  'cuts',
  'rebuilds',
  'helps',
  'enables',
  'makes',
  'uses',
])

const JARGON = new Set([
  'api',
  'app',
  'architecture',
  'backend',
  'cache',
  'ci',
  'database',
  'deployment',
  'frontend',
  'framework',
  'github',
  'javascript',
  'nextjs',
  'react',
  'repository',
  'schema',
  'server',
  'supabase',
  'tailwind',
  'typescript',
  'vercel',
])

function isRecord(value: unknown): value is Record<string, unknown> {
  return typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value)
}

function pathLabel(path: string, field: string): string {
  return path ? `${path}.${field}` : field
}

function requiredString(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is string {
  if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.trim().length === 0) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a non-empty string`)
    return false
  }
  if (PLACEHOLDER.test(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} contains placeholder text`)
    return false
  }
  return true
}

function stringArray(value: unknown, path: string, errors: string[]): boolean {
  if (!Array.isArray(value) || value.length === 0) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a non-empty array`)
    return false
  }
  value.forEach((item, index) => {
    requiredString(item, `${path}[${index}]`, errors)
  })
  return value.every((item) => typeof item === 'string' && item.trim().length > 0)
}

function validUrl(value: unknown, path: string, errors: string[]): value is string {
  if (!requiredString(value, path, errors)) return false
  try {
    const url = new URL(value)
    if (url.protocol !== 'https:' && url.protocol !== 'http:') {
      errors.push(`${path} must use http or https`)
      return false
    }
    return true
  } catch {
    errors.push(`${path} must be an absolute URL`)
    return false
  }
}

function validDate(value: unknown, path: string, errors: string[]): value is string {
  if (!requiredString(value, path, errors)) return false
  if (!ISO_DATE.test(value) || Number.isNaN(Date.parse(`${value}T00:00:00Z`))) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a real date in YYYY-MM-DD form`)
    return false
  }
  return true
}

function verificationSource(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is VerificationSource {
  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a verification source`)
    return false
  }

  if (value.kind === 'live-url' || value.kind === 'public-repo') {
    const urlOk = validUrl(value.url, pathLabel(path, 'url'), errors)
    const dateOk = validDate(value.checkedOn, pathLabel(path, 'checkedOn'), errors)
    return urlOk && dateOk
  }

  if (value.kind === 'user-confirmed') {
    const noteOk = requiredString(value.note, pathLabel(path, 'note'), errors)
    const dateOk = validDate(
      value.confirmedOn,
      pathLabel(path, 'confirmedOn'),
      errors,
    )
    return noteOk && dateOk
  }

  errors.push(`${path}.kind must be live-url, public-repo, or user-confirmed`)
  return false
}

function verifiedClaim(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is VerifiedClaim {
  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a verified claim`)
    return false
  }
  const claimOk = requiredString(value.claim, `${path}.claim`, errors)
  const sourceOk = verificationSource(value.source, `${path}.source`, errors)
  return claimOk && sourceOk
}

function figure(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is CaseStudyFigure {
  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a figure`)
    return false
  }
  const srcOk = requiredString(value.src, `${path}.src`, errors)
  const altOk = requiredString(value.alt, `${path}.alt`, errors)
  const captionOk = requiredString(value.caption, `${path}.caption`, errors)
  return srcOk && altOk && captionOk
}

function footnote(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is CaseStudyFootnote {
  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be a footnote`)
    return false
  }
  const markerOk = requiredString(value.marker, `${path}.marker`, errors)
  const textOk = requiredString(value.text, `${path}.text`, errors)
  return markerOk && textOk
}

function externalLink(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
): value is CaseStudyExternalLink {
  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be an external link`)
    return false
  }
  const labelOk = requiredString(value.label, `${path}.label`, errors)
  const urlOk = validUrl(value.url, `${path}.url`, errors)
  return labelOk && urlOk
}

function optionalArray(
  value: unknown,
  path: string,
  errors: string[],
  validateItem: (item: unknown, itemPath: string, errors: string[]) => boolean,
): boolean {
  if (value === undefined) return true
  if (!Array.isArray(value)) {
    errors.push(`${path} must be an array when present`)
    return false
  }
  value.forEach((item, index) => validateItem(item, `${path}[${index}]`, errors))
  return true
}

function words(value: string): string[] {
  return value.toLowerCase().match(/[a-z0-9]+(?:[-'][a-z0-9]+)*/g) ?? []
}

function jargonCount(value: string): number {
  return words(value).filter((word) => JARGON.has(word)).length
}

export function validateCaseStudies(input: unknown): ContentValidationResult {
  const errors: string[] = []
  const warnings: string[] = []

  if (!Array.isArray(input)) {
    throw new Error('Content validation failed:\n- caseStudies must be an array')
  }

  const slugs = new Set<string>()

  input.forEach((entry, index) => {
    const path = `caseStudies[${index}]`
    if (!isRecord(entry)) {
      errors.push(`${path} must be an object`)
      return
    }

    // Destructure before narrowing. A type guard applied to `entry.slug` does not
    // persist across later property reads, so the checked value must be bound once.
    const { slug, tagline, problem, response, outcome, verified, year } = entry

    if (requiredString(slug, `${path}.slug`, errors)) {
      if (!SLUG.test(slug)) {
        errors.push(`${path}.slug must be lowercase kebab-case`)
      }
      if (slugs.has(slug)) errors.push(`${path}.slug must be unique`)
      slugs.add(slug)
    }

    const taglineOk = requiredString(tagline, `${path}.tagline`, errors)
    const problemOk = requiredString(problem, `${path}.problem`, errors)
    const responseOk = requiredString(response, `${path}.response`, errors)
    requiredString(outcome, `${path}.outcome`, errors)
    stringArray(entry.deliverables, `${path}.deliverables`, errors)
    stringArray(entry.technologies, `${path}.technologies`, errors)

    if (!Array.isArray(verified) || verified.length === 0) {
      errors.push(`${path}.verified must contain at least one verified claim`)
    } else {
      verified.forEach((claim, claimIndex) => {
        verifiedClaim(claim, `${path}.verified[${claimIndex}]`, errors)
      })
    }

    optionalArray(entry.figures, `${path}.figures`, errors, figure)
    optionalArray(entry.footnotes, `${path}.footnotes`, errors, footnote)
    optionalArray(entry.externalLinks, `${path}.externalLinks`, errors, external

# 5.1 — Accessibility
**Layer:** 5 — Quality and launch · **Depends on:** 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4 · **Blocks:** 5.4

## Objective

Verify that the ten-point floor in `design/system.md` §8 held across every route, theme, and viewport.

This plan does not *add* accessibility. Accessibility is merge-blocking from commit one — every plan in Layers 1 through 4 shipped its own share of the floor, in the same commit as the feature it belonged to. 2.4 shipped the skip link, focus management, and landmarks. 2.2 shipped reduced motion alongside the reveal primitive. 1.3 shipped a contrast-verified token layer. 4.2 shipped SSR with self-referential canonicals. This plan is the audit that proves those commitments survived contact with four layers of feature work.

**Consequently: anything this plan finds is a regression, not a backlog item.** There is no triage step, no severity split, no "ship it and file a follow-up." A failure means a specific commit broke a floor that was previously green, and the fix is to correct that commit before merge. The acceptance criteria below are written with zero deferral paths on purpose.

The floor exists because the studied source material failed it comprehensively:

| Defect observed | Floor point that prevents it |
|---|---|
| `#94A3B8`-class text at **3.19:1, used 99 times** on ~11px type — the most-used text color on the page, failing AA | 1 |
| **Four infinite animation loops**, no `prefers-reduced-motion` handling | 5 |
| **No skip link, no `focus-visible`**, behind a tall multi-tier fixed header | 2, 3 |
| Heading order `h1 → h3 → h2` | 4 |
| Content-bearing images with empty `alt` | 6 |
| A CSR portfolio **canonicalizing 13 case studies to its homepage** | 8 |

Every one of those is cheap to prevent at authoring time and expensive to retrofit. That asymmetry is the entire argument for holding the floor from commit one rather than scheduling it here.

## Prerequisites

- 4.1–4.4 complete. All routes render; production build green.
- **Route inventory frozen.** Every concrete URL, not route templates — each case study is its own page. Sourced from the generated sitemap and cross-checked against the App Router tree.
- Both themes selectable pre-paint (class on `<html>`, no flash, no post-hydration switch).
- `scripts/verify-tokens.mjs` (plan 1.3) exits 0 on the current tree — all 20 contrast pairs.
- Playwright + Chromium installed. NVDA installed (Windows 11 — NVDA is the screen reader for this project; nothing here assumes JAWS or VoiceOver).
- Browser zoom at 100%, OS text scaling at 100%, NVDA settings at default before the manual passes.

## Steps

### 1 — Freeze the route inventory

Emit `tests/fixtures/routes.json` from the sitemap: every concrete URL, expected `h1`, expected canonical, expected landmark set, and expected interactive controls in DOM order.

CI compares this against the built route manifest and **fails on any drift in either direction** — a route missing from the inventory is untested, and a route in the inventory that no longer builds is stale. This is what stops a future case study from merging without entering the accessibility matrix.

### 2 — Automated: axe-core in CI, every route, both themes

`@axe-core/playwright` as a devDependency. The matrix is *routes × {light, dark} × {desktop 1280, mobile 390}*.

```ts
// tests/accessibility.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';
import routes from './fixtures/routes.json' assert { type: 'json' };

const THEMES = ['light', 'dark'] as const;
const VIEWPORTS = [
  { name: 'desktop', width: 1280, height: 900 },
  { name: 'mobile',  width: 390,  height: 844 },
];

for (const route of routes) {
  for (const theme of THEMES) {
    for (const vp of VIEWPORTS) {
      test(`a11y ${route.path} [${theme}/${vp.name}]`, async ({ page }) => {
        await page.setViewportSize({ width: vp.width, height: vp.height });
        await page.addInitScript(t => {
          document.documentElement.classList.add(t);
          localStorage.setItem('theme', t);
        }, theme);
        await page.goto(route.path, { waitUntil: 'networkidle' });

        const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
          .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa', 'wcag21a', 'wcag21aa'])
          .analyze();

        // No .exclude(), no .disableRules(). An exclusion is a silent waiver.
        expect(results.violations, JSON.stringify(results.violations, null, 2)).toEqual([]);
      });
    }
  }
}
```

Companion assertions in the same file, covering the floor points axe cannot judge:

```ts
test(`floor ${route.path} [${theme}]`, async ({ page }) => {
  // (2) skip link is the FIRST focusable element, and it works
  await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
  const first = page.locator(':focus');
  await expect(first).toHaveText(/skip to main/i);
  await first.press('Enter');
  await expect(page.locator('main')).toBeFocused();

  // (4) one h1; heading order never skips
  const levels = await page.$$eval('h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6', ns =>
    ns.map(n => Number(n.tagName[1])));
  expect(levels.filter(l => l === 1)).toHaveLength(1);
  expect(levels[0]).toBe(1);
  levels.reduce((prev, cur) => {
    expect(cur - prev).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1); // descend by any amount, ascend by one
    return cur;
  });

  // (9) landmarks present; every nav named
  await expect(page.locator('header')).toHaveCount(1);
  await expect(page.locator('main')).toHaveCount(1);
  await expect(page.locator('footer')).toHaveCount(1);
  for (const nav of await page.locator('nav').all()) {
    const name = await nav.getAttribute('aria-label')
              ?? await nav.getAttribute('aria-labelledby');
    expect(name, 'every nav needs an accessible name').toBeTruthy();
  }

  // (3) focus-visible: 2px --focus ring, 2px offset, on EVERY interactive element
  const focusColor = await page.evaluate(() =>
    getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--focus').trim());
  for (const el of await page.locator(
    'a[href], button, input, select, textarea, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])').all()) {
    await el.focus();
    const s = await el.evaluate(n => {
      const c = getComputedStyle(n);
      return { w: c.outlineWidth, o: c.outlineOffset, style: c.outlineStyle, col: c.outlineColor };
    });
    expect(s.style).not.toBe('none');   // never outline:none without a replacement
    expect(s.w).toBe('2px');
    expect(s.o).toBe('2px');
  }

  // (6) alt text
  for (const img of await page.locator('img').all()) {
    const alt = await img.getAttribute('alt');
    expect(alt, 'img must carry an alt attribute').not.toBeNull();
    if (alt === '') {
      // decorative: alt="" AND aria-hidden
      await expect(img).toHaveAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    }
  }

  // (8) SSR + correct self-referential canonical, checked on the raw HTML
  const html = await (await page.request.get(route.path)).text();
  expect(html).toContain(route.h1);                       // content present pre-hydration
  const canonicals = [...html.matchAll(/<link[^>]+rel="canonical"[^>]+href="([^"]+)"/g)];
  expect(canonicals).toHaveLength(1);
  expect(canonicals[0][1]).toBe(route.canonical);         // self-referential, absolute
});
```

Reduced motion (point 5) gets its own context, because the assertion is *visible*, not *un-animated*:

```ts
test.describe('reduced motion', () => {
  test.use({ reducedMotion: 'reduce' });
  test(`content visible ${route.path}`, async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto(route.path);
    for (const el of await page.locator('.reveal').all()) {
      await expect(el).toBeVisible();
      const s = await el.evaluate(n => getComputedStyle(n));
      expect(s.opacity).toBe('1');            // not merely un-animated — VISIBLE
      expect(s.transform).toBe('none');
    }
    const infinite = await page.evaluate(() =>
      [...document.querySelectorAll('*')].filter(n =>
        getComputedStyle(n).animationIterationCount === 'infinite').length);
    expect(infinite).toBe(0);
  });
});
```

**CI job** — `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:

```yaml
  accessibility:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: build
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 20, cache: npm }
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx playwright install --with-deps chromium
      - run: node scripts/verify-tokens.mjs        # 1.3 token contract, re-asserted
      - run: npm run build
      - run: npx next start -p 3000 &
      - run: npx wait-on http://localhost:3000
      - run: npx playwright test tests/accessibility.spec.ts
      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        if: failure()
        with: { name: a11y-report, path: playwright-report/ }
```

**Automated tooling catches roughly 30% of real accessibility defects.** Axe detects a missing `alt` attribute; it cannot tell you the alt text is wrong. It flags a contrast ratio; it cannot tell you the tab order is nonsensical. It validates ARIA syntax; it cannot tell you the announcement is confusing. That 30% figure is the whole reason steps 4 and 5 are mandatory release evidence rather than optional exploratory QA — a green axe run is a floor on the floor, not proof of it.

### 3 — Contrast: token layer *and* point of use

**3a. Re-run the token contract.** `node scripts/verify-tokens.mjs` — all 20 pairs, unmodified from 1.3. The relevant floor:

| Pair | Light | Dark |
|---|---|---|
| `--muted-2` / `--paper` | 4.80 | 5.16 |
| `--muted-2` / `--surface` | **4.51** | 4.86 |

The light `--muted-2` on `--surface` pairing is the tightest tolerance in the system. **It passes by 0.01.** There is no headroom for rounding, no headroom for an inherited `opacity`, no headroom for a `mix-blend-mode`, no headroom for a filter, and no headroom for compositing over anything that is not exactly `--surface`. No alpha-modified text anywhere — this is what point 1 forbids outright.

**3b. Sample the rendered pages.** A token can pass in isolation and still be composed onto the wrong background at point of use. `--muted-2` label text is authored against `--paper`; drop it inside a `MortarGrid` cell and it is now on `--surface`, spending the entire 0.01 margin. Nest it once more inside anything tinted and it fails silently — and it will fail on the *most-used* text color, which is precisely how the source material ended up with a 3.19:1 color 99 times.

So sample the actual pixels:

```ts
// walk every text node, resolve the PAINTED background, not the assumed one
const failures = await page.evaluate(() => {
  const srgb = (c: number) => (c <= 0.03928 ? c / 12.92 : ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4);
  const lum = ([r, g, b]: number[]) =>
    0.2126 * srgb(r / 255) + 0.7152 * srgb(g / 255) + 0.0722 * srgb(b / 255);
  const parse = (s: string) => s.match(/[\d.]+/g)!.map(Number);
  const ratio = (f: number[], b: number[]) => {
    const [hi, lo] = [lum(f), lum(b)].sort((a, z) => z - a);
    return (hi + 0.05) / (lo + 0.05);
  };

  const out: any[] = [];
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('*')) {
    const text = [...el.childNodes]
      .filter(n => n.nodeType === 3 && n.textContent!.trim()).map(n => n.textContent!.trim()).join(' ');
    if (!text) continue;
    const cs = getComputedStyle(el);
    const fg = parse(cs.color);

    // alpha-modified text is banned outright, regardless of ratio
    let alpha = fg[3] ?? 1;
    for (let n: HTMLElement | null = el; n; n = n.parentElement) alpha *= Number(getComputedStyle(n).opacity);
    if (alpha < 1) { out.push({ text, reason: 'alpha-modified text', alpha }); continue; }

    // climb until an actually-painted background is found
    let bg = [255, 255, 255], node: HTMLElement | null = el;
    for (; node; node = node.parentElement) {
      const c = parse(getComputedStyle(node).backgroundColor);
      if ((c[3] ?? 1) > 0) { bg = c; break; }
