# 1.3 — Token layer
**Layer:** 1 — Foundation · **Depends on:** 1.2 · **Blocks:** 1.4, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4

## Objective
Every value in `design/system.md` exists exactly once, as a CSS custom property, in a file under `styles/tokens/`. Tailwind's theme is a projection of those properties, overridden rather than extended, so that a color, size, or spacing value outside the system cannot compile into a class name. `app/globals.css` no longer contains the glassmorphism and gradient utilities it currently ships. After this plan, a component can only express the design system, and 1.4's linter exists to catch the remaining escape hatches rather than the bulk of the problem.

## Prerequisites

- 1.2 complete: the tree builds green, `tailwindcss` is in `devDependencies`, and `app/globals.css` still holds the old `glass-card` / `gradient-text` / `cyber-gradient` layer that this plan deletes.
- `tailwind.config.js` still contains the 31-entry purple/pink/violet/fuchsia `safelist` and an `hsl(var(--x))` color map from the shadcn defaults. Both are replaced wholesale here, not edited.
- **Resolved before writing code — four ambiguities in `design/system.md`.** Each is settled in the step that implements it; recorded here so no later plan re-opens them:
  1. `--accent-fill` and `--accent-text` resolve to the same ramp step, so the primary button has no in-ramp hover. Resolved in Step 3 with `--accent-fill-hover`.
  2. `--band-y: 48px / 64px` is written as two values with no breakpoint rule. Resolved in Step 5.
  3. `darkMode: ["class"]` cannot distinguish explicit-light from no-preference. Resolved in Step 2.
  4. `.mortar > *` has no border for `.lift` to colorize on hover. Resolved in Step 7.
  5. `--t-h2` had two conflicting definitions — `39.06px` in the fixed scale table and a `2rem` (32px) fluid maximum. Resolved in Step 4: the clamps ramp two scale steps each and terminate on their table values. `design/system.md` was corrected to match.

## Steps

### Step 1 — File layout

One concern per file. `globals.css` becomes an import manifest plus base element styles, nothing else.

```bash
cd "C:/Users/anubh/Downloads/Antigravity Projects/saake-dev"
mkdir -p styles/tokens
```

```
styles/
  tokens/
    color.css      raw ramps + semantic mapping, both modes
    type.css       faces, scale, leading, tracking, measure
    space.css      spacing scale, containers, rhythm
    motion.css     two duration bands, two curves
    surface.css    radius, grain, mortar
app/
  globals.css      imports the above, then base element styles
```

The split is not cosmetic. 1.4's linter reads `styles/tokens/*.css` to build the set of defined tokens, and treats everything else as a consumer. A token defined outside that directory is invisible to the linter and therefore forbidden.

### Step 2 — The theme contract

**Resolves ambiguity 3.** Three states are visible to the user — light, dark, follow-system. But `darkMode: ["class"]` gives CSS one bit, so "explicitly light" and "no preference on a light OS" become indistinguishable, and there is no way to write a rule that means *the user chose light*.

The contract:

- **`localStorage.theme` holds the three-state preference:** `"light" | "dark" | "system"`. Absent means `"system"`.
- **`<html data-theme>` always holds the *resolved* value:** `"light"` or `"dark"`. Never absent, never `"system"`. An inline script in `<head>` resolves preference against `prefers-color-scheme` and writes the attribute before first paint.
- CSS therefore only ever sees a concrete mode. `[data-theme="light"]` genuinely means light.

This is why the attribute is always written rather than omitted for the default: an absent attribute would push the system-dark case into a media query, forcing every dark token to be declared twice and putting the Tailwind `dark:` variant out of step with the custom properties. Always-resolved keeps one declaration site per value.

The inline script and the toggle UI belong to **2.4**. The token layer only depends on the attribute being present, and carries a `prefers-color-scheme` fallback for the no-JS case so that a reader without JavaScript still gets a coherent theme.

The same inline script sets `html.js`, which the reveal primitive in 2.2 gates on. One script, two attributes.

### Step 3 — `styles/tokens/color.css`

Two tiers. The **raw** tier holds every literal hex in the system and is mode-independent — it is a palette, not a theme. The **semantic** tier maps raw values onto roles and is the only tier a component may reference. This is `design/system.md` §1's "Semantic tokens only in components" made mechanical: Step 9 does not expose the raw tier to Tailwind at all, so `text-a-4` is not a class that can exist.

The mapping tier is also where P2's split-token move happens. Because `--accent-text` and `--accent-fill` are separate names pointing at the same raw step, either can move later without touching the other.

