# 2.3 — Composites

**Layer:** 2 — System in code · **Depends on:** 2.1, 2.2 · **Blocks:** 2.4, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4

## Objective

The five components that carry actual content — `MortarGrid`, `ProjectCard`, `SpecTable`, `Figure`, `Footnote` — plus the two-variant `Button`. After this plan the mortar technique (P1 corollary) is the site's only card-grid implementation, `SpecTable` is the only way metadata reaches the page, and every card grid gets hairline seams with no per-cell border management. Layer 4 assembles pages out of these; it does not build new ones.

## Prerequisites

- 2.1 shipped: `Rule`, `Label`, `SectionHead`, `Prose`, `ExternalLink`.
- 2.2 shipped: `.lift`, `Reveal`, and the reduced-motion block.
- 3.2's case-study type does not exist yet. `ProjectCard` here takes a flat prop interface; 3.2 defines the schema and 4.2 maps schema → props. Do not block on the schema and do not invent it.

## Steps

### 1. `MortarGrid` — hairline seams from one container

The technique: paint the container `--rule`, set `gap: 1px`, paint the children `--surface`. The gap bleeds through as a perfect 1px seam. No cell manages a border, no two adjacent cells produce a 2px double edge, and the outer edge is a `1px solid --rule` on the container itself.

The children must be opaque. Any cell that fails to paint `--surface` shows a `--rule`-colored block, which is why the fill is applied by the container's own child selector rather than trusted to each cell.

```css
/* app/globals.css — @layer components */
@layer components {
  .mortar {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .mortar > * {
    background: var(--surface);
    /* Present so .lift has an edge to shift. See 2.2 step 7. */
    border: 1px solid transparent;
  }
}
```

The `1px solid transparent` on every cell is the resolution of the note carried from 2.2. Without it, `.lift:hover { border-color: ... }` on a mortar cell sets a color on a zero-width border and nothing appears. With it, the hover edge draws inside the cell, one pixel in from the seam — which reads correctly, because the seam belongs to the grid and the hover state belongs to the cell. It also means cells are 2px smaller in each axis than the naive calculation; that is uniform across all cells and invisible.

Columns are set per breakpoint. Below 1024 everything is single-column — §3 forbids half-collapsed grids.

```tsx
// components/composites/mortar-grid.tsx
import type { ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

export interface MortarGridProps {
  children: ReactNode
  /** Columns at ≥1024px. Below that the grid is always single-column. */
  columns?: 2 | 3 | 4
  /** Render as ul; children must then be li. Use for lists of things. */
  as?: 'div' | 'ul'
  className?: string
}

const COLS: Record<2 | 3 | 4, string> = {
  2: 'lg:grid-cols-2',
  3: 'lg:grid-cols-3',
  4: 'lg:grid-cols-4',
}

export function MortarGrid({
  children,
  columns = 2,
  as: Tag = 'div',
  className,
}: MortarGridProps) {
  return (
    <Tag
      className={cn('mortar grid-cols-1', COLS[columns], className)}
      {...(Tag === 'ul' ? { role: 'list' } : null)}
    >
      {children}
    </Tag>
  )
}
```

`role="list"` is explicit because the `ul` carries `display: grid`, which strips the implicit list role in Safari/VoiceOver — the same defect handled for `Prose` lists in 2.1.

The `lg:` prefix must be in the class string literally, not composed (`` `lg:grid-cols-${n}` ``), or Tailwind's content scanner will not emit the class. The `COLS` lookup table exists for exactly that reason.

### 2. `Button` — two variants, no third

`primary` is `--accent-fill` background with `--paper` text (6.70 light / 5.58 dark). `ghost` is transparent with a 1px `--rule` border and `--ink` text, edge shifting to `--accent-edge` on hover. Both are sharp. Neither is mono — the label register explicitly excludes buttons.

`Button` renders `<button>`; `ButtonLink` renders `<a>` or a Next `<Link>`. They are separate exports rather than an `as` prop because their required props differ and conflating them is how a `<button href>` gets shipped.

```tsx
// components/composites/button.tsx
import type { ReactNode } from 'react'
import Link from 'next/link'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

type Variant = 'primary' | 'ghost'

const BASE =
  'inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 ' +
  'font-sans font-medium transition-colors duration-[160ms] ' +
  'disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-100'

function variantStyle(variant: Variant): React.CSSProperties {
  if (variant === 'primary') {
    return {
      background: 'var(--accent-fill)',
      color: 'var(--paper)',
      border: '1px solid var(--accent-fill)',
      fontSize: 'var(--t-body-s)',
      lineHeight: 1.6,
    }
  }
  return {
    background: 'transparent',
    color: 'var(--ink)',
    border: '1px solid var(--rule)',
    fontSize: 'var(--t-body-s)',
    lineHeight: 1.6,
  }
}

export interface ButtonProps {
  children: ReactNode
  variant?: Variant
  type?: 'button' | 'submit' | 'reset'
  onClick?: () => void
  disabled?: boolean
  /** Required when the button's content is an icon alone. */
  'aria-label'?: string
  className?: string
}

export function Button({
  children,
  variant = 'ghost',
  type = 'button',
  onClick,
  disabled,
  className,
  ...aria
}: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      type={type}
      onClick={onClick}
      disabled={disabled}
      aria-label={aria['aria-label']}
      className={cn(BASE, `btn-${variant}`, className)}
      style={variantStyle(variant)}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}

export interface ButtonLinkProps {
  children: ReactNode
  href: string
  variant?: Variant
  /** External destinations get target/rel and the ↗ from ExternalLink instead. */
  className?: string
}

export function ButtonLink({
  children,
  href,
  variant = 'ghost',
  className,
}: ButtonLinkProps) {
  return (
    <Link
      href={href}
      className={cn(BASE, `btn-${variant}`, className)}
      style={variantStyle(variant)}
    >
      {children}
    </Link>
  )
}
```

Hover states are CSS, so they cost no JS and stay inside the `(hover: hover)` gate:

```css
@layer components {
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .btn-ghost:hover { border-color: var(--accent-edge); }
    .btn-primary:hover { background: var(--accent-text); border-color: var(--accent-text); }
  }
  .btn-primary:disabled,
  .btn-ghost:disabled { color: var(--muted-2); border-color: var(--rule); background: transparent; }
}
```

`--accent-fill` and `--accent-text` resolve to the same ramp step (`a-5` light, `a-3` dark), so the primary hover is currently a no-op color change. That is correct and deliberate: the primary button's hover feedback is the focus/active affordance and the cursor, not a brightness shift, because a brightness shift needs a ramp step that would break the "never write an accent value outside the ramp" rule. If the ramp later separates fill from text, the hover becomes real with no code change.

Disabled state uses `--muted-2` (4.80:1) rather than opacity. `disabled:opacity-100` in `BASE` explicitly defeats any framework default that would drop opacity — the system has no alpha-modified text.

**No third variant.** If a page seems to need one, it needs a link.

### 3. `ProjectCard`

A mortar cell. Mono metadata above, display title, plain-language description, and a terminal mono stack fragment — the jargon gradient (P13) rendered as layout: the technical payload is physically last and typographically separate, so a non-technical reader stops before it.

The whole card is clickable via a stretched-link overlay on the title, which keeps the accessible name on the heading link and avoids nesting interactive elements. External verification links inside the card sit above the overlay with `relative z-10`.

```tsx
// components/composites/project-card.tsx
import Link from 'next/link'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'
import { Label, ExternalLink } from '@/components/primitives'

export interface ProjectCardProps {
  /** Internal case-study route, e.g. /work/skillbridge */
  href: string
  title: string
  /** Noun-phrase tagline. No finite verb. (P15) */
  tagline: string
  /** Plain-language, jargon-free. (P13) */
  description: string
  /** Terminal fragment: the stack, comma-separated. Rendered mono, last. */
  stack?: string[]
  /** Mono metadata, e.g. "2024" or "Solo". */
  meta?: string
  /** Live deployment. Routes the visitor off-site to verify. (P18) */
  liveUrl?: string
  /** Public source. (P18) */
  repoUrl?: string
  className?: string
}

export function ProjectCard({
  href,
  title,
  tagline,
  description,
  stack,
  meta,
  liveUrl,
  repoUrl,
  className,
}: ProjectCardProps) {
  const titleId = `project-${href.replace(/\W+/g, '-')}`

  return (
    <article
      className={cn('lift group relative flex flex-col gap-4 p-8', className)}
    >
      {meta && <Label>{meta}</Label>}

      <h3
        id={titleId}
        className="font-display"
        style={{
          fontSize: 'var(--t-h3)',
          lineHeight: 1.3,
          letterSpacing: 'var(--track-tight)',
          color: 'var(--ink)',
        }}
      >
        <Link href={href} className="after:absolute after:inset-0">
          {title}
        </Link>
      </h3>

      <p
        style={{
          fontSize: 'var(--t-body-s)',
          lineHeight: 1.6,
          color: 'var(--ink-2)',
        }}
      >
        {tagline}
      </p>

      <p
        style={{
          fontSize: 'var(--t-body-s)',
          lineHeight: 1.6,
          color: 'var(--muted)',
        }}
      >
        {description}
      </p>

      {(liveUrl || repoUrl) && (
        <div className="relative z-10 mt-auto flex flex-wrap gap-6 pt-2">
          {liveUrl && (
            <ExternalLink href={liveUrl} mono>
              Live
            </ExternalLink>
          )}
          {repoUrl && (
            <ExternalLink href={repoUrl} mono>
              Source
            </ExternalLink>
          )}
        </div>
      )}

      {stack && stack.length > 0 && (
        <Label as="p" className="pt-2">
          {stack.join(' · ')}
        </Label>
      )}
    </article>
  )
}
```

`after:absolute after:inset-0` on the title link stretches its hit area over the whole positioned `article`. One focusable element per card by default, named by the title. The external links sit above it on `z-10`, so they are separately reachable and separately announced.

`.lift` is on the article, and the mortar cell's `1px solid transparent` border from step 1 is what the hover edge shifts. Focus-within on the card fires when the title link is focused, giving the keyboard user the same edge signal (2.2 step 7).

The status dot — the one rounded element in the system — belongs on live-status indicators in the work index, not here. It ships in 4.2 where the live-check data from 3.3 exists. Do not add a placeholder for it.

