# 2.2 — Motion

**Layer:** 2 — System in code · **Depends on:** 1.3, 2.1 · **Blocks:** 2.3, 2.4, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4

## Objective

One entrance, one interaction, and neither can be reimplemented. A single `.reveal` class driven by a single shared IntersectionObserver handles every entrance on the site; a single `.lift` class handles every hover. The `html.js` gate ships in the same commit, so no-JS and pre-hydration users see content rather than nothing, and the reduced-motion block ships in the same commit, so a user who asked for stillness gets visible content rather than a blank page. Framer Motion is not involved in either.

## Prerequisites

- 1.3 shipped `--dur-enter: 640ms`, `--ease-enter: cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)`, `--dur-act: 160ms`, `--ease-act: ease`, `--accent-edge`, `--rule`.
- 2.1 shipped, so there are primitives to wrap.
- `app/layout.tsx` exists and controls `<html>` — the gate script must be in `<head>` before any paint.

## Steps

### 1. The `html.js` gate

This is the load-bearing detail and it goes first. The hidden state of `.reveal` is scoped under `html.js`. The class is set by a blocking inline script in `<head>`, before the browser paints anything. If JS is off, or the script fails, or the user is looking at pre-hydration HTML, `.js` is never set, the hidden state never applies, and the content is simply visible with no animation.

The inverse — shipping `opacity: 0` unconditionally and removing it in a `useEffect` — is the bug in the source material. It renders an invisible page to every crawler that doesn't execute JS and to every user during the hydration window.

```tsx
// components/motion/js-gate.tsx
export function JsGate() {
  return (
    <script
      // Sets html.js before first paint. The .reveal hidden state is scoped
      // under .js, so without this the content renders visible and unanimated.
      dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
        __html: `document.documentElement.classList.add('js')`,
      }}
    />
  )
}
```

Mounted as the first child of `<head>` in `app/layout.tsx` (wired in 2.4, alongside the theme script — the two are adjacent and both must be pre-paint):

```tsx
<html lang="en" suppressHydrationWarning>
  <head>
    <JsGate />
    {/* ThemeScript — 2.4 */}
  </head>
  <body>...</body>
</html>
```

`suppressHydrationWarning` on `<html>` is required: the server emits `<html lang="en">` and the client sees `<html lang="en" class="js">`. Without it React logs a mismatch on every load. It suppresses the warning for `<html>` attributes only, not for the tree.

Both inline scripts are static string literals with no interpolation. Nothing user-supplied ever enters them.

### 2. The reveal CSS

Straight from `design/system.md` §4. Every value is fixed: 18px travel, 640ms, the token curve, five delay slots at 80ms increments.

```css
/* app/globals.css — @layer components */
@layer components {
  html.js .reveal {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(18px);
    transition:
      opacity var(--dur-enter) var(--ease-enter),
      transform var(--dur-enter) var(--ease-enter);
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }

  html.js .reveal.in {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    will-change: auto;
  }

  .rd-1 { transition-delay:  80ms; }
  .rd-2 { transition-delay: 160ms; }
  .rd-3 { transition-delay: 240ms; }
  .rd-4 { transition-delay: 320ms; }
  .rd-5 { transition-delay: 400ms; }
}
```

Three things about this block.

The hidden state is `html.js .reveal` but `.in` is scoped the same way — a `.reveal.in` outside `.js` would be a no-op rule with nothing to override, and keeping both under the gate means the entire animated state machine is absent when JS is.

`will-change` is set on the hidden state and released on `.in`. A permanent `will-change` on every revealable element on a long page holds a compositor layer per element for the life of the document. Releasing it at the end of the one-shot is the whole point of a one-shot.

The delay classes are unscoped. `transition-delay` on an element with no transition does nothing, so they are inert without `.js` and there is no reason to gate them.

### 3. Reduced motion — same commit, non-negotiable

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }

  /* Content must be VISIBLE, not merely un-animated. */
  html.js .reveal {
    opacity: 1 !important;
    transform: none !important;
    will-change: auto !important;
  }
}
```

The distinction the second block enforces: the universal `transition-duration: .01ms` alone makes the reveal instant, but the element still starts at `opacity: 0` and still depends on the observer firing to reach `opacity: 1`. If the observer never fires — element already above the fold in a browser with a quirk, script error, an element inside a container that never intersects — the content stays invisible forever for exactly the users least able to work around it. Setting the visible state directly removes the dependency.

The `!important` flags are load-bearing here and are the only sanctioned use of `!important` in the codebase. Document that in the 1.4 lint config as a scoped exception for this media block.

`scroll-behavior: auto` also disables the smooth in-page scrolling that the skip link would otherwise use (2.4).

### 4. The observer

One observer instance for the whole document, created lazily on first subscription, shared by every revealable element. Not one observer per component — a case-study page has forty reveal targets and forty observers is forty times the bookkeeping for identical options.

```ts
// components/motion/reveal-observer.ts
const THRESHOLD = 0.15

let observer: IntersectionObserver | null = null

function getObserver(): IntersectionObserver | null {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') return null
  if (typeof IntersectionObserver === 'undefined') return null

  if (!observer) {
    observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const entry of entries) {
          if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue
          entry.target.classList.add('in')
          observer?.unobserve(entry.target) // one-shot
        }
      },
      { threshold: THRESHOLD },
    )
  }

  return observer
}

/** Observe an element. Returns a cleanup that unobserves it. */
export function observeReveal(el: Element): () => void {
  const io = getObserver()

  // No IntersectionObserver (very old browser, some test environments):
  // reveal immediately rather than leaving the element hidden.
  if (!io) {
    el.classList.add('in')
    return () => {}
  }

  io.observe(el)
  return () => io.unobserve(el)
}
```

The `if (!io)` branch is the same principle as the reduced-motion block: every failure path resolves to visible.

`unobserve` on first intersection is what makes this one-shot. Elements do not re-hide on scroll-up, the observer's tracked set shrinks as the user reads, and a long page ends with an observer watching nothing.

### 5. The hook

```ts
// components/motion/use-reveal.ts
'use client'

import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react'
import { observeReveal } from './reveal-observer'

/**
 * Attach to any element to reveal it on first intersection.
 * The element must also carry the `reveal` class.
 */
export function useReveal<T extends HTMLElement = HTMLDivElement>() {
  const ref = useRef<T>(null)

  useEffect(() => {
    const el = ref.current
    if (!el) return

    // Already revealed (e.g. Fast Refresh re-run) — do not re-hide.
    if (el.classList.contains('in')) return

    return observeReveal(el)
  }, [])

  return ref
}
```

### 6. The `Reveal` component

The interface most call sites use. `index` drives the stagger and cycles `i % 5 + 1`, so a list of forty items uses five delay slots and never invents a sixth.

```tsx
// components/motion/reveal.tsx
'use client'

import type { ElementType, ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'
import { useReveal } from './use-reveal'

const DELAY_SLOTS = 5

export interface RevealProps {
  children: ReactNode
  /**
   * Stagger position within a group. Cycles modulo 5 so unbounded lists
   * never exceed the five defined slots. Omit for no delay.
   */
  index?: number
  /** Element to render. Default div. */
  as?: Extract<ElementType, 'div' | 'section' | 'article' | 'li' | 'header' | 'footer'>
  className?: string
}

export function Reveal({
  children,
  index,
  as: Tag = 'div',
  className,
}: RevealProps) {
  const ref = useReveal<HTMLDivElement>()
  const delay =
    index === undefined ? undefined : `rd-${(index % DELAY_SLOTS) + 1}`

  return (
    <Tag ref={ref} className={cn('reveal', delay, className)}>
      {children}
    </Tag>
  )
}
```

`(index % 5) + 1` on a non-negative index yields 1–5. Callers pass array indices; nothing passes a negative number, and no guard is added for a case that cannot occur.

Usage:

```tsx
{projects.map((project, i) => (
  <Reveal key={project.slug} index={i} as="li">
    <ProjectCard {...project} />
  </Reveal>
))}
```

### 7. The `Lift` wrapper and the `.lift` class

One hover gesture site-wide (P8): 2px up, border to `--accent-edge`, 160ms, gated behind `@media (hover: hover)` so a touch device never lands in a stuck hover state.

```css
@layer components {
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .lift {
      transition:
        transform var(--dur-act) var(--ease-act),
        border-color var(--dur-act) var(--ease-act);
    }
    .lift:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
      border-color: var(--accent-edge);
    }
  }

  /* Keyboard parity: the same edge shift, without the translate. */
  .lift:focus-visible,
  .lift:focus-within {
    border-color: var(--accent-edge);
  }
}
```

`:focus-within` covers the common shape — a bordered card whose only focusable child is the title link. Without it a keyboard user tabbing through a grid gets a focus ring on the link but no state change on the card that contains it. The translate is deliberately omitted for focus: moving an element while a focus ring sits on it makes the ring appear to drift.

`.lift` requires the element to already have a border. It sets `border-color`, not `border`, because the mortar grid (2.3) paints its seams via the container background and its children have no border of their own — applying `.lift` to a mortar cell shifts a border that isn't there. In the mortar case, cells get an explicit `1px solid transparent` border so the hover has a surface to act on. That is a 2.3 concern; note it here so it is not discovered there.

```tsx
// components/motion/lift.tsx
import type { ElementType, ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

export interface LiftProps {
  children: ReactNode
  as?: Extract<ElementType, 'div' | 'article' | 'li' | 'a'>
  className?: string
}

/** Hover affordance. The element must carry a border for the edge shift to read. */
export function Lift({ children, as: Tag = 'div', className }: LiftProps) {
  return <Tag className={cn('lift', className)}>{children}</Tag>
}
```

`Lift` is a server component — pure class application, no state. Most call sites will apply `.lift` directly via `cn()` on an existing element rather than adding a wrapper div; the component exists for the cases where wrapping is cleaner. Both are correct. What is not correct is any second hover gesture.

### 8. Barrel and Framer boundary

```ts
// components/motion/index.ts
export { Reveal } from './reveal'
export { Lift } from './lift'
export { useReveal } from './use-reveal'
export { JsGate } from './js-gate'
```

`observeReveal` is not exported from the barrel. It is an implementation detail and exporting it invites a second consumer with different options.

Framer Motion stays in the tree for page transitions only. Add the boundary as a lint rule in 1.4's ESLint config so it fails CI rather than review:

```js
