# 2.1 — Primitives

**Layer:** 2 — System in code · **Depends on:** 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 · **Blocks:** 2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4

## Objective

The token layer becomes usable. Six primitives — `Rule`, `Label`, `SectionHead`, `Prose`, `Grain`, `ExternalLink` — exist as typed React components that read tokens and nothing else, so no page ever writes a color, a size, or a tracking value again. After this plan, the label register (P5) is a component rather than a convention, and the only way to get a divider or an eyebrow on this site is to import one. Every subsequent plan composes these rather than re-deriving them.

## Prerequisites

- 1.3 shipped: `--paper` `--surface` `--rule` `--rule-strong` `--muted-2` `--muted` `--ink-2` `--ink`, the accent semantics (`--accent-text` `--accent-fill` `--accent-edge` `--accent-wash` `--focus`), `--t-*`, `--track-*`, `--measure`, `--grain`, `--radius`, `--dur-*`, `--ease-*` all resolve in both modes.
- 1.4 shipped: `next/font/local` faces exposed as `--font-display` (Instrument Serif), `--font-sans` (Geist Sans), `--font-mono` (Geist Mono), mapped into Tailwind as `font-display`, `font-sans`, `font-mono`.
- Tailwind `theme.extend` maps tokens to utilities: `text-ink`, `text-muted-2`, `bg-surface`, `border-rule`, `text-label`, `tracking-label`, and so on. Utilities in this plan assume that mapping. Where no utility exists, use inline `style` with `var(--token)` — never an arbitrary Tailwind value.
- `cn()` helper (clsx + tailwind-merge) at `lib/cn.ts`.

## Steps

### 1. Create the primitives barrel

`components/primitives/index.ts` re-exports every primitive. Pages import from `@/components/primitives`, never from a file path. This is what makes the CI rule in 1.4 ("no component defines a color") enforceable by inspection — the surface area is one directory.

```ts
// components/primitives/index.ts
export { Rule } from './rule'
export { Label } from './label'
export { SectionHead } from './section-head'
export { Prose } from './prose'
export { Grain } from './grain'
export { ExternalLink } from './external-link'
```

### 2. `Rule` — the 1px divider

The only sanctioned horizontal separator. Renders `<hr>` by default so it carries a native separator role; renders a `<div role="separator">` when vertical. `strong` swaps to `--rule-strong` for the rare emphasis edge. There is no thickness prop — a 2px rule is not in the system.

```tsx
// components/primitives/rule.tsx
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

export interface RuleProps {
  /** Vertical divider. Requires a height from the parent (flex/grid stretch). */
  orientation?: 'horizontal' | 'vertical'
  /** Use --rule-strong instead of --rule. For emphasis edges only. */
  strong?: boolean
  className?: string
}

export function Rule({
  orientation = 'horizontal',
  strong = false,
  className,
}: RuleProps) {
  const color = strong ? 'var(--rule-strong)' : 'var(--rule)'

  if (orientation === 'vertical') {
    return (
      <div
        role="separator"
        aria-orientation="vertical"
        className={cn('w-px self-stretch shrink-0', className)}
        style={{ backgroundColor: color }}
      />
    )
  }

  return (
    <hr
      className={cn('h-px w-full border-0', className)}
      style={{ backgroundColor: color }}
    />
  )
}
```

Note the `border-0` + background approach rather than `border-t`. A bordered `<hr>` picks up UA margin and box-model quirks across browsers; a 1px block with a background is exactly 1px everywhere, which matters when a rule sits flush against a mortar seam.

### 3. `Label` — the mono register

P5 in a component. Geist Mono, uppercase, `--t-label`, `+0.16em`, `--muted-2`. Every one of those five properties is fixed. The component takes no size, no weight, no color prop — if a label needs to be bigger, it is not a label.

`as` covers the real uses: `span` (default), `div`, `p`, `dt`, `th`, `figcaption`, `legend`. `tone="accent"` is the single sanctioned deviation, used for active nav and live status, and it moves to `--accent-text` which is 6.70/5.58 — still above the floor.

```tsx
// components/primitives/label.tsx
import type { ElementType, ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

type LabelTone = 'muted' | 'ink' | 'accent'

export interface LabelProps {
  children: ReactNode
  /** Semantic element. Default span. */
  as?: Extract<
    ElementType,
    'span' | 'div' | 'p' | 'dt' | 'dd' | 'th' | 'figcaption' | 'legend'
  >
  /** muted (default, --muted-2) · ink (--ink-2, for table headers) · accent (--accent-text, active/live only) */
  tone?: LabelTone
  className?: string
}

const TONE: Record<LabelTone, string> = {
  muted: 'var(--muted-2)',
  ink: 'var(--ink-2)',
  accent: 'var(--accent-text)',
}

export function Label({
  children,
  as: Tag = 'span',
  tone = 'muted',
  className,
}: LabelProps) {
  return (
    <Tag
      className={cn('font-mono uppercase', className)}
      style={{
        fontSize: 'var(--t-label)',
        lineHeight: 1.4,
        letterSpacing: 'var(--track-label)',
        color: TONE[tone],
      }}
    >
      {children}
    </Tag>
  )
}
```

Uppercasing is done in CSS, not in the content. Screen readers read `text-transform` output as the underlying string, so "Selected work" stays a word rather than an initialism.

### 4. `SectionHead` — number + label + h2

The repeating section opener: a mono section number, an optional mono eyebrow, and the heading. The number is decorative structure, not content, so it is `aria-hidden`; the accessible name of the section comes from the heading alone.

`level` exists because heading order never skips (a11y floor 4). A section inside a case study body may need `h3`. The component will not render an `h1` — page titles are written directly, one per page.

```tsx
// components/primitives/section-head.tsx
import type { ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'
import { Label } from './label'

export interface SectionHeadProps {
  /** The heading text. */
  children: ReactNode
  /** Section number, e.g. "01". Rendered mono, aria-hidden. */
  number?: string
  /** Mono eyebrow above the heading. */
  eyebrow?: string
  /** Optional standfirst below the heading, --t-lead, --muted. */
  lead?: ReactNode
  /** h2 (default) or h3. Never h1. */
  level?: 2 | 3
  /** Attach an id so a section can be aria-labelledby this heading. */
  id?: string
  className?: string
}

export function SectionHead({
  children,
  number,
  eyebrow,
  lead,
  level = 2,
  id,
  className,
}: SectionHeadProps) {
  const Heading = (level === 2 ? 'h2' : 'h3') as 'h2' | 'h3'
  const sizeToken = level === 2 ? 'var(--t-h2)' : 'var(--t-h3)'
  const leading = level === 2 ? 1.2 : 1.3

  return (
    <div className={cn('flex flex-col gap-3', className)}>
      {(number || eyebrow) && (
        <div className="flex items-baseline gap-4">
          {number && (
            <Label aria-hidden="true" className="tabular-nums">
              {number}
            </Label>
          )}
          {eyebrow && <Label>{eyebrow}</Label>}
        </div>
      )}

      <Heading
        id={id}
        className="font-display"
        style={{
          fontSize: sizeToken,
          lineHeight: leading,
          letterSpacing: 'var(--track-tight)',
          color: 'var(--ink)',
          textWrap: 'balance',
        }}
      >
        {children}
      </Heading>

      {lead && (
        <p
          style={{
            fontSize: 'var(--t-lead)',
            lineHeight: 1.5,
            letterSpacing: '-0.005em',
            color: 'var(--muted)',
            maxWidth: 'var(--measure)',
          }}
        >
          {lead}
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  )
}
```

`Label` does not declare `aria-hidden` in its props interface. Add it:

```ts
export interface LabelProps {
  // ...existing
  'aria-hidden'?: boolean | 'true' | 'false'
}
```

and spread it onto `Tag`. Keep the prop surface explicit rather than extending `HTMLAttributes` — an open prop bag is how `style={{ color: '#fff' }}` gets past review.

### 5. `Prose` — measure-constrained rich text

Wraps any block of running text. Sets the measure, the body size, and the descendant styles for `p`, `a`, `strong`, `em`, `ul`, `ol`, `blockquote`, `code`. This is the only place descendant selectors are allowed, because it is the only component that receives content it did not author.

Tailwind Typography is not used. It ships its own scale, its own color ramp, and its own `max-width`, all of which contradict the token layer, and disabling them costs more configuration than writing the twelve rules directly.

```tsx
// components/primitives/prose.tsx
import type { ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/lib/cn'

export interface ProseProps {
  children: ReactNode
  /** default 68ch · narrow 62ch for long-form case studies */
  measure?: 'default' | 'narrow'
  /** body (--t-body, 20px) · small (--t-body-s, 16px) for asides and captions blocks */
  size?: 'body' | 'small'
  className?: string
}

export function Prose({
  children,
  measure = 'default',
  size = 'body',
  className,
}: ProseProps) {
  return (
    <div
      className={cn('prose-block', className)}
      style={{
        maxWidth: measure === 'narrow' ? '62ch' : 'var(--measure)',
        fontSize: size === 'body' ? 'var(--t-body)' : 'var(--t-body-s)',
        lineHeight: size === 'body' ? 1.65 : 1.6,
        color: 'var(--ink)',
      }}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  )
}
```

The descendant rules live in `app/globals.css` under `@layer components`, not in the component, so they cascade to MDX output as well:

```css
@layer components {
  .prose-block > * + * { margin-block-start: 24px; }

  .prose-block strong { font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
  .prose-block em     { font-style: italic; }

  .prose-block a {
    color: var(--accent-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
    text-decoration-color: var(--rule-strong);
    transition: text-decoration-color var(--dur-act) var(--ease-act);
  }
  .prose-block a:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--accent-edge); }

  .prose-block ul,
  .prose-block ol { padding-inline-start: 24px; }
  .prose-block ul { list-style: none; }
  .prose-block ol { list-style: decimal; }
  .prose-block li + li { margin-block-start: 8px; }
  .prose-block ul > li::before {
    content: "";
    display: inline-block;
    inline-size: 8px;
    block-size: 1px;
    vertical-align: 0.35em;
    margin-inline-start: -24px;
    margin-inline-end: 16px;
    background: var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .prose-block blockquote {
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-inline-start: 24px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-lead);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .prose-block code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 2px 4px;
  }

  .prose-block h2,
  .prose-block h3,
  .prose-block h4 { font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--ink); }
  .prose-block h2 { font-size: var(--t-h2); line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: var(--track-tight); margin-block-start: 48px; }
  .prose-block h3 { font-size: var(--t-h3); line-height: 1.3; letter-spacing: var(--track-tight); margin-block-start: 32px; }
  .prose-block h4 { font-size: var(--t-body); line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500; margin-block-start: 24px; }
}
```

The unordered-list bullet is a 1px dash in `--rule-strong`, not a disc. It is the same hairline vocabulary as everything else, and it is the reason `list-style: none` is set rather than restyled. `list-style: none` on a styled `ul` removes the list role in Safari with VoiceOver — add `role="list"` to any `ul` inside `Prose` authored in TSX, and configure the MDX pipeline (3.2) to emit it.

### 6. `Grain` — the fixed texture layer

One element, fixed, top of stack, `pointer-events: none`, opacity from `--grain` so it re-tunes it
